// Probe 26 — Cluster I consumer border: compound assignment `s += vec[i] ?? "x"`
//
// Compound assignment `+=` on text lowers via `OpAppendText` (the same
// op that consumes from the eval stack in cluster I's failing pattern).
// But the *initial* `s` variable is a function-scope text local, not the
// block-local `__ncc_N`.  The question: when the consumer is "append to
// `s`," does the cluster I dangling-Str fire?
//
// Two possible outcomes:
//   (a) FAIL like 02 — OpAppendText reads the dangling Str from the
//       stack regardless of which target buffer it appends INTO; the
//       cluster I fix must cover this consumer too.
//   (b) PASS — `+=` lowers through a different value-block path
//       (perhaps the `OpAppendText` op already does an implicit
//       materialisation, similar to format strings's `OpFormatText`
//       behaviour pre-cluster-III).
//
// If (a), the fix surface is "any consumer of a text value-block."
// If (b), there's a path-specific escape that informs cluster I's fix
// shape (could materialise via the consumer-side rather than scope-side).
//
// Pairing: diff against 02 (assignment-from-scratch) and 20 (direct print
// argument).
// Status: cluster I consumer-border probe; informs fix-shape choice.

struct H { items: vector<text> }

fn main() {
  h = H { items: [] };
  h.items += "world";
  s = "hello ";
  s += h.items[0] ?? "fallback";
  assert(s == "hello world", "probe 26: got '{s}', want 'hello world'");
  println("probe 26 PASSED");
}
